  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "The query is a polar double prodrug (ethyl ester plus hydroxyamidine) with an azetidine amide and benzyl amidoxime; the closest visible structural context, AZD0837, uses the same amidoxime–azetidine motif and has measured human absolute oral bioavailability of 22–55%, above the F≥20% threshold.",
      "card_ids": ["C03","C06","C23","C31"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Prodrug active-metabolite contexts also support F≥20%: perindoprilat after oral perindopril was 30±6% in cirrhotic patients and 35±17% in elderly subjects, while perindopril itself was 65–95%.",
      "card_ids": ["C19","C29","C24","C07","C11","C13","C14","C39"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "All visible L1 oral-bioavailability records in transferable contexts are at or above 20% (betaxolol 80–89%, perindopril 65–75%, encainide 76–78%, vernakalant ~60%, atenolol ~51%, perindoprilat 30±6%), so there is no affirmative evidence for F<20%.",
      "card_ids": ["C02","C05","C07","C10","C18","C19","C22"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L3 oral exposure records show measurable systemic exposure after oral dosing, including active-metabolite exposure from an ethyl ester prodrug (enalaprilat after oral enalapril), consistent with oral absorption; they do not quantify F but do not contradict the high prediction.",
      "card_ids": ["C42","C48","C49","C52","C51","C43","C50"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L5 records are for enalapril/enalaprilat and atenolol, not the query; they show variable permeability (enalapril Caco-2 Papp 0.28×10^-6 vs hiPSC IEC 2.95×10^-6 cm/s; enalaprilat human jejunal P_eff 1.3×10^-6 cm/s; atenolol low-permeability marker without secretory-dominant efflux) and do not provide affirmative threshold-relevant evidence for F<20%.",
      "card_ids": ["C66","C67","C70","C74","C75","C77","C68","C72"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "gut_wall_efflux_intestinal_metabolism",
      "applicability": "low",
      "direction": "neutral_or_unclear",
      "decision_effect": "no_change",
      "card_ids": ["C66","C67","C68","C69","C70","C71","C72","C73","C74","C75","C76","C77"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct oral bioavailability, absorption, permeability, solubility, gut-wall efflux, or intestinal metabolism measurement for the query molecule itself is present in the visible cards.",
    "L5 records are for enalapril/enalaprilat and atenolol, not the query; no query-matched gut-wall efflux or intestinal metabolism data are available.",
    "L3 AUC/Cmax records lack intravenous comparators and cannot be converted into absolute F values.",
    "The closest analog AZD0837 has a lower F bound of 22%, close to the 20% threshold.",
    "First-pass conversion of the ethyl ester and hydroxyamidine prodrug moieties in the query is not directly evidenced.",
    "The physicochemical prior is mixed/unclear and does not provide affirmative low-F evidence."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "Predicted high (F≥20%) because the query is a double prodrug analogous to AZD0837 (human absolute F 22–55%) and prodrug active-metabolite contexts are above threshold; all visible L1 F records are ≥20%, L3 evidence is supportive/neutral, L4 is unrelated/neutral, and L5 provides no query-matched gut-wall efflux or intestinal-metabolism evidence that would affirmatively support F<20%. Confidence remains moderate because no direct query measurement is present and the AZD0837 lower bound is close to the threshold."
}

